    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">The gap most executives miss</h2>
      <p>The problem is rarely your experience. It is how your materials and stories present that experience to the market.</p>
      <p>Your resume lists deliverables instead of organizational decisions. Your LinkedIn reads like a team lead, not a VP. Your interview answers go deep on execution — what you built, what you shipped, how you scaled the system — instead of wide on strategy, leadership leverage, and the decisions only you could have made at that scope.</p>
      <p>Hiring managers and executive search firms evaluating VP and C-suite candidates are asking a different set of questions: What is this person's organizational impact? Do they think at the level the role requires? Can they lead at scale, communicate to the board, own a P&L? If your materials do not answer those questions, you are being evaluated at the wrong level.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">What executive coaching actually changes</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Positioning at the organizational level, not the functional level — scope, decisions, and impact that read at VP or C-suite</li>
        <li>Stories that demonstrate P&L thinking, stakeholder alignment, and leadership leverage — not individual execution</li>
        <li>Interview answers calibrated to the right altitude for VP and C-suite evaluators, including executive presentations and multi-round loops</li>
        <li>Offer strategy built for complex executive compensation packages — equity, RSUs, title, scope, and cash components</li>
        <li>Market signal that moves the market proactively, rather than waiting for inbound from the same sources that have been reaching out for years</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

